WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.606
Probability asks how likely possible data is under a specified parameter. Likelihood uses the same numerical

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:15.013
expression but treats observed data as fixed while comparing candidate parameters. It does not become a

00:00:15.013 --> 00:00:17.790
normalized probability distribution over those candidates.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.896
Likelihood varies the parameter while holding the actual data fixed. It is not a probability distribution

00:00:24.896 --> 00:00:25.720
over parameters.

00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:32.433
The product assumes conditionally independent Bernoulli observations with one shared success probability.

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:38.158
Why would a prediction of near certainty be bad when both outcomes have been observed? Pause

00:00:38.158 --> 00:00:40.443
here and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:52.487
The logarithm preserves the location of a maximum. Negating it turns likelihood maximization into loss minimization.

00:00:52.487 --> 00:01:00.677
For our starting inputs, Likelihood: 0.0625. Total NLL: 2.7726. Mean NLL: 0.69315.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.928
The observed success fraction minimizes this loss. Moving toward certainty at either endpoint is punished because

00:01:07.928 --> 00:01:09.610
both outcome types occurred.

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:15.383
State conditional-independence assumptions and whether your reported loss is a sum or mean.

